        "content": "```json\n{\n  \"supplement_requests\": [\n    {\n      \"gap_id\": \"phase3_rct_registry_verification\",\n      \"gap_question\": \"Are there any ongoing or completed but unpublished Phase III randomized controlled trials of microbiome interventions (FMT, probiotics, defined consortia) combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumour patients registered in ClinicalTrials.gov or WHO ICTRP?\",\n      \"success_criteria\": \"Confirmation of absence or identification of specific trial registry numbers (NCT identifiers) with Phase III status, solid tumour indications, microbiome intervention arms, and ICI combinations. If absent, document the search strategy, date, and filters used.\",\n      \"intended_use\": \"background\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH04\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ],\n      \"targeted_queries\": [\n        {\n          \"query_type\": \"keyword\",\n          \"query_text\": \"ClinicalTrials.gov phase III fecal microbiota transplantation immune checkpoint inhibitor solid tumor\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"query_type\": \"keyword\",\n          \"query_text\": \"WHO ICTRP microbiome intervention phase 3 cancer immunotherapy\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"gap_id\": \"human_intratumoral_metabolite_absence\",\n      \"gap_question\": \"Do any published studies report absolute concentrations (μmol/g tissue or μM) of microbiome-derived metabolites (inosine, short-chain fatty acids, bile acids) measured directly in human tumour tissue or tumour interstitial fluid from immune checkpoint inhibitor-treated patients, correlated with clinical response?\",\n      \"success_criteria\": \"Identification of specific studies with paired tumour tissue metabolomics and ICI outcome data, OR confirmation that such measurements are explicitly stated as lacking in systematic reviews or methodological papers. Must distinguish between fecal/serum measurements (already documented) and intratumoral measurements (the gap).\",\n      \"intended_use\": \"quantification\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH02\",\n        \"CH03\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ],\n      \"targeted_queries\": [\n        {\n          \"query_type\": \"question\",\n          \"query_text\": \"What are the absolute physiological concentrations of inosine and short-chain fatty acids measured directly in human solid tumour tissue samples from patients treated with immune checkpoint inhibitors, and how do these levels correlate with treatment response?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"query_type\": \"keyword\",\n          \"query_text\": \"tumour interstitial fluid metabolomics immune checkpoint inhibitor human paired stool serum tissue\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"gap_id\": \"strain_function_experimental_validation\",\n      \"gap_question\": \"Beyond human strain-level metagenomic associations, are there any studies that experimentally validate the specific functions of ICI-associated bacterial strains (e.g., via culturomics, gnotobiotic mouse models with isolated strains, or metabolite measurement from specific strains) in the context of immune checkpoint inhibitor response?\",\n      \"success_criteria\": \"Identification of studies that isolate specific strains identified in human cohorts and validate their function through experimental models, OR documentation that such validation is lacking despite strain-level associations being established. Must distinguish between genomic potential inference and experimental functional validation.\",\n      \"intended_use\": \"mechanism\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH02\",\n        \"CH03\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ],\n      \"targeted_queries\": [\n        {\n          \"query_type\": \"keyword\",\n          \"query_text\": \"gnotobiotic mouse model isolated bacterial strain immune checkpoint inhibitor functional validation\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"query_type\": \"question\",\n          \"query_text\": \"Have specific bacterial strains associated with immune checkpoint inhibitor response in human cohorts been isolated and experimentally validated for their functional effects on anti-tumour immunity?\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"directed_reads\": [\n    {\n      \"paper_id\": \"82ebda249f3476e1948f523a9219bd678f5165c3\",\n      \"question_material\": [\n        {\n          \"question_id\": \"Q_timing_specificity\",\n          \"examples\": [\n            {\n              \"attribution\": \"Zhang et al. (2026)\",\n              \"conditions\": \"Multi-centre retrospective cohort (n=21,108) across six academic medical centres in California. Antibiotic exposure defined as ≥2 days of systemic oral or IV antibiotics with known microbiome impact. Exposure windows: pre-only (-60 to -1 days before ICI), post-only (+1 to +60 days after ICI initiation).\",\n              \"finding\": \"Post-only antibiotic exposure significantly increased mortality (HR 1.27; 95% CI 1.20–1.35) after multivariable adjustment for demographics, cancer type, treatment line, and baseline health indicators (albumin, NLR, recent hospitalization). Pre-only exposure showed no significant long-term survival association (HR 1.04; 95% CI 0.99–1.10). Combined pre+post exposure had highest mortality (HR 1.31; 95% CI 1.23–1.40).\",\n              \"reference_ids\": [],\n              \"use_in_review\": \"Provide quantitative evidence for the critical window hypothesis: antibiotic exposure within 60 days after ICI initiation is more detrimental than pre-ICI exposure, supporting the biological rationale that microbiome integrity during early T-cell activation is essential.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"explanation\": \"This study establishes the temporal specificity of antibiotic-ICI interactions. The finding that post-ICI exposure carries greater risk than pre-ICI exposure challenges prior literature focus on pre-treatment antibiotics. The authors hypothesize this reflects the critical window for tumour-reactive T-cell expansion and priming, which depends on microbiome-derived signals. This temporal pattern supports mechanistic models where microbiome signals are required during early immune activation rather than merely for baseline immune competence.\",\n          \"remaining_points\": [\n            \"Retrospective design cannot establish causality; unmeasured confounders (e.g., infection severity, ECOG status) may influence results.\",\n            \"No direct microbiome sequencing data; mechanism inferred from clinical outcomes.\",\n            \"Subgroup analyses by cancer type have limited power for rare cancers (often <300 per window).\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:252309032\",\n      \"question_material\": [\n        {\n          \"question_id\": \"Q_inosine_mechanism\",\n          \"examples\": [\n            {\n              \"attribution\": \"Li et al. (2019) / CheckMate 025 re-analysis\",\n              \"conditions\": \"Patients with advanced renal cell carcinoma treated with nivolumab (n=394) or everolimus (n=349). Baseline plasma inosine levels stratified by median into high vs. low groups. Outcome: overall survival (OS).\",\n              \"finding\": \"High baseline plasma inosine associated with longer OS specifically in nivolumab arm (median OS 33 months vs. 22 months for high vs. low groups). No significant association in everolimus control arm. Mechanistically, inosine directly inhibits tumour cell UBA6, enhancing MHC-I expression and tumour immunogenicity rather than acting primarily on T cells.\",\n              \"reference_ids\": [\n                \"R15\"\n              ],\n              \"use_in_review\": \"Provide human clinical correlation between a microbiome-associated metabolite (inosine) and ICI efficacy, with specific survival data. Establishes tumour-intrinsic mechanism (UBA6 inhibition) as distinct from immune cell-focused mechanisms.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"explanation\": \"This study identifies inosine as a microbiome-derived metabolite that enhances ICI response through a tumour-intrinsic mechanism. The specificity to the nivolumab arm (not everolimus) supports an immune-mediated effect. The UBA6 inhibition mechanism represents a novel pathway distinct from T-cell-focused mechanisms commonly described. However, the study did not directly measure inosine concentrations in the human clinical cohort due to sample limitations, relying instead on UBA6 expression as a downstream proxy.\",\n          \"remaining_points\": [\n            \"Human cohort (n=22) did not have direct plasma inosine measurements; relied on public RCC dataset for metabolite-survival analysis.\",\n            \"Small validation cohort limits statistical power.\",\n            \"Inosine has multiple sources (microbiome, diet, host enzymes); attribution to microbiome is indirect.\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"paper_id\": \"071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a\",\n      \"question_material\": [\n        {\n          \"question_id\": \"Q_FMT_efficacy_toxicity\",\n          \"examples\": [\n            {\n              \"attribution\": \"FMT-LUMINate Phase 2 Trial\",\n              \"conditions\": \"NSCLC cohort (n=20, first-line, PD-L1 TPS ≥50%, no prior anti-PD-1) received FMT (single oral capsule from healthy donor) followed by pembrolizumab monotherapy. Melanoma cohort (n=20, first-line, allowed prior adjuvant anti-PD-1 if >6 months off) received FMT followed by nivolumab + ipilimumab (dual ICI).\",\n              \"finding\": \"NSCLC ORR 80% (16/20), exceeding 64% primary endpoint threshold. Melanoma ORR 75% (15/20). In melanoma dual ICI cohort, 65% experienced grade 3+ AEs (13/20), with diarrhoea/colitis most common (20%) and myocarditis in 15%. Median onset of severe toxicity was 40 days, earlier than literature-reported 60 days. No grade 3+ AEs in NSCLC monotherapy cohort.\",\n              \"reference_ids\": [],\n              \"use_in_review\": \"Provide specific efficacy (ORR) and safety (grade 3+ AE rates, specific toxicities, onset timing) data for FMT+ICI combinations. Demonstrate ICI regimen-dependent toxicity: dual ICI carries higher risk than monotherapy.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"explanation\": \"This Phase 2 trial provides the most robust clinical evidence for FMT enhancing ICI efficacy in specific contexts. The stark contrast in toxicity between NSCLC (monotherapy, no grade 3+ AEs) and melanoma (dual ICI, 65% grade 3+ AEs) demonstrates that FMT safety is highly dependent on the ICI regimen background. The early onset of toxicity (40 days vs. expected 60 days) and association with Prevotella-enriched donors suggest specific microbiome-ICI interactions that require careful monitoring. These data support cautious optimism for FMT in monotherapy settings while highlighting significant risks in combination ICI contexts.\",\n          \"remaining_points\": [\n            \"Single-arm design without control group; efficacy compared to historical controls.\",\n            \"Small sample size (n=20 per cohort).\",\n            \"Donor selection not standardized; toxicity linked to specific donor microbiome features.\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to analyze the current chapter needs and identify high-value supplement requests and directed reads that would materially improve the writer plan for this microbiome-ICI review.\n\nLooking at the level1 and level2 tool results, I can see several gaps have been partially fulfilled or remain unmet:\n\n1. **Phase III RCT absence (case_E_phase3_rct / phase3_rct_absence)**: This gap has been partially fulfilled. The meta-analysis (5af3aee4ca5f3778325d44c8432c8b71b95ca531) confirms no completed large-scale Phase III RCTs in published literature up to September 2024, but registry searches are still needed to confirm absence of ongoing/unpublished trials.\n\n2. **Intratumoral metabolite quantification (case_C2_inosine_tme / intratumoral_metabolite_quantification)**: This remains partially fulfilled. We have fecal SCFA concentrations (Nomura et al. 2020: fecal propionic acid median 172.6 μmol/g in responders vs 103.6 μmol/g in non-responders) and plasma inosine data (CheckMate 025: high vs low groups with median OS 33 vs 22 months), but NO direct intratumoral concentration measurements in human tumor tissue. Multiple searches confirm this data is lacking.\n\n3. **Strain-level functional validation (strain_level_functional_validation)**: This has been fulfilled in round 3. Gunjur et al. provides human strain-level metagenomic data linked to ICI outcomes, showing strain-level resolution improves prediction over species-level. However, experimental functional validation (culturomics, gnotobiotic models) is still lacking.\n\nKey observations from the materials:\n- The shared outline already states \"补充检索确认目前无已完成的微生物群干预 ICI 治疗实体瘤的 III 期随机对照试验\" - this is supported by the meta-analysis.\n\n\n- Human tumor tissue metabolite concentrations remain a critical evidence gap that multiple searches have failed to resolve.\n- Strain-level associations now have human cohort support, though mechanistic validation through experimental models is still needed.\n\nFor chapter-specific needs:\n\n**CH01 (Clinical Evidence)**: The antibiotic timing data (Zhang et al. n=21,108, HR 1.27 for post-ICI exposure) and FMT trial data (FMT-LUMINate ORR 80% NSCLC, 75% melanoma with 65% grade 3+ AE in dual ICI) are well-covered. The Phase III absence is partially confirmed but could benefit from direct registry verification.\n\n**CH02 (Microbial Features)**: Strain-level data is now available (Gunjur et al.), and SCFA fecal concentrations are documented (Nomura et al.). The intratumoral metabolite gap persists.\n\n**CH03 (Mechanisms)**: The inosine-UBA6 mechanism is well-documented (Li et al. 2019/CorpusId:252309032), but human tumor tissue concentration data is missing.\n\n**CH04 (Interventions)**: FMT trial data is available, Phase III status is partially confirmed.\n\n**CH05 (Limitations)**: This chapter would benefit most from the remaining gaps - particularly the intratumoral metabolite data absence and Phase III trial registry confirmation.\n\nI should prioritize supplement requests that address these specific chapter needs rather than pursuing broad searches.\n\nFor directed reads, I need to focus on papers already identified in the available materials that contain specific quantitative data or mechanistic details relevant to the gaps. The Zhang et al. antibiotic timing paper, Nomura et al. SCFA quantification, Li et al. inosine-UBA6 mechanism, and Gunjur et al. strain-level data all have relevant content already flagged in the materials. I should extract the specific quantitative values and mechanistic details from these papers rather than searching for new sources.\n\nFor supplements, I'm identifying three high-value gaps: confirming Phase III trial absence through direct registry searches, documenting the lack of human intratumoral metabolite measurements, and clarifying what strain-level functional validation exists versus what remains experimental. Each targets a specific claim in the review that needs stronger